{"name":"Colored / circle contact lenses","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Multipel_colored_pencils_14.jpg/960px-Multipel_colored_pencils_14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Multipel_colored_pencils_14.jpg","caption":"Multipel colored pencils 14","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":36,"casual":{"lead":["Coloured and circle lenses are cosmetic contacts that change the look of your iris, either by tinting it or by adding a ring that makes the eye appear larger. In forums they are mostly used to lighten dark eyes, and the change shows the moment they go in.","A printed pigment layer covers or modifies the natural iris colour, and circle lenses add a darker or larger outer rim to enlarge the visible iris. No studies were found that measure how much they change perceived attractiveness, so that part rests on user reports."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People get fitted by an eye-care professional, then wear them for the day like regular contacts, choosing a shade or diameter for a natural or dramatic look. Forum users compare shades, wear time and how natural the result looks."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["The FDA classes decorative lenses as medical devices needing a prescription and lists corneal abrasion, allergy, reduced vision and infection that can lead to blindness, especially from lenses obtained without a proper fitting. A case report notes that some circle lenses contain metals that can matter during MRI."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Multipel_colored_pencils_14.jpg/960px-Multipel_colored_pencils_14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Multipel_colored_pencils_14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_